            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="0" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test has shown air leakage from the following house components:

- Receptacles

- Front door

- Furnace chimney

- Bathroom fan

During the infiltration test, we notice a high level of air leakage coming from the furnace chimney at the junction of the attic, this section needs to be sealed properly. Also, we notice a similar airflow from the bathroom fan, in this case, we recommend the replacement of the unit with a better-sealed unit. These 2 situations can easily lower the temperature during cold and representing a high level of heat loss, which will lead you to high energy consumption to keep the space warm and comfortable.



The other elements listed below can be fixed by adding weatherstripping. The outdoor light was visible from the exterior front door. This situation will also contribute to the air leakage present in the house.



</airSeal>
                <ceilings>At the time of the audit, the attic insulation inspection was not possible. We estimated the insulation level according to the year of construction. It doesn?????????t seem to be possible to add more insulation because of the shape of the roof and the available space. During the inspection, we notice an ice bridge building up on the edge of the roof (the cathedral ceiling section, the lower section of the slope). This might be a sign of heat loss through the ceiling that gets condensed and turning the snow into ice.



The same situation has been seen on the opposite side above the bathroom. This issue might be caused by the heat loss escaping from the bathroom ceiling fan.



We highly recommend the inspection of the roof and attic by a specialist. 

</ceilings>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
